faq:
  - q: Up to what age are tantrums normal in children?
    a: Tantrums start in most children at around 18 months and are very common in the toddler years. They thin out as a child learns to put feelings into words, and drop noticeably by about four. If they are still frequent, long and intense after five, or if they also happen at school, it is worth talking to a professional.
  - q: My child hits and throws things when angry. What should I do?
    a: 'Safety comes first: move your child and anyone nearby out of harm''s way. Teaching a rule during a tantrum does not work, because a child in that state cannot take it in. Set the limit calmly and briefly — "You can be angry, you cannot hit." The conversation happens afterwards, once your child has settled.'
  - q: Is it right to ignore a tantrum?
    a: 'It depends. Where the tantrum is aimed at getting something, not giving in but staying nearby works. Where your child is frightened, overwhelmed or exhausted, being ignored only adds to it. The distinction is this: do not give in to the demand, but do not leave your child alone with it.'
  - q: Are tantrums a sign of a behavioural disorder?
    a: 'Usually not. At an age where they are expected, they are an ordinary part of development. What makes them worth an assessment is the pattern: several a day, lasting longer than fifteen to twenty minutes, your child hurting themselves or others, no reduction after the age of five, and the same picture appearing both at home and at school.'
  - q: Can anger be linked to ADHD or anxiety?
    a: It can. Anger is not a diagnosis, it is a signal. Difficulty with impulse control, anxiety, disrupted sleep, learning difficulties and sensory sensitivity all raise it. To tell these apart you may find the articles on [ADHD symptoms](/en/blog/what-is-adhd-symptoms-in-children.html) and [anxiety in children](/en/blog/anxiety-and-separation-anxiety-in-children.html) useful.
  - q: Where can I start in Famagusta?
    a: Our [child psychology](/en/services/child-psychology.html) service covers sessions with the child, and [parent counselling](/en/blog/parent-counselling.html) covers sessions with parents alone. Where anger is concerned, the two usually run together. If you are not sure which one fits, we can look at it in a first conversation. Sessions can be held in English or Turkish.
